  <img decoding="async" width="768" height="432" src="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-768x432-1.jpg" class="attachment-medium_large size-medium_large wp-post-image" alt="IRS Modifies Crypto Question on Tax Form — Now Focusing on Taxable Cryptocurrency Transactions" loading="lazy" style="float:left; margin:0 15px 15px 0;" srcset="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-768x432-1.jpg 768w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-300x169.jpg 300w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-1024x576.jpg 1024w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-696x385.jpg 696w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-1068x601.jpg 1068w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-747x420.jpg 747w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-190x107.jpg 190w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-380x214.jpg 380w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-760x428.jpg 760w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs.jpg 1280w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 768px) 100vw, 768px" /></p> 
  
  <p>
    <strong>The U.S. Internal Revenue Service (IRS) has modified the crypto question asked on the main U.S. tax form. Reducing the scope of the question, the IRS now focuses on taxable cryptocurrency transactions.</strong>
  </p>
  
  <h2>
    New Crypto Question on Tax Form 1040
  </h2>
  
  <p>
    The IRS published a draft Form 1040 for the tax year 2021 Thursday. Form 1040 is the main tax form used for filing individual income tax returns in the U.S. The draft form shows that the tax agency has modified the crypto question slightly.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    The crypto question now reads: “At any time during 2021, did you receive, sell, exchange, or otherwise dispose of any financial interest in any virtual currency?”
  </p>
  
  <p>
    Previously, the question read: “At any time during 2020, did you receive, sell, send, exchange, or otherwise acquire any financial interest in any virtual currency?”
  </p><figure id="attachment_476877" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-476877" style="width: 1173px" class="wp-caption aligncenter">
  
  <img decoding="async" srcset="https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-tax-form-300x116.jpg 300w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-tax-form-768x297.jpg 768w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-tax-form-1024x396.jpg 1024w, https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-tax-form.jpg 1173w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1173px) 100vw, 1173px" loading="lazy" class="wp-image-476877 size-full" src="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-tax-form.jpg" alt="" width="1173" height="454" /><figcaption id="caption-attachment-476877" class="wp-caption-text">Draft IRS Form 1040 for the year 2021.</figcaption></figure> 
  
  <p>
    For the year 2021, the IRS has removed the word “send” and replaced “acquire” with “disposed of.”
  </p><figure id="attachment_476879" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-476879" style="width: 940px" class="wp-caption aligncenter">
  
  <img decoding="async" srcset="https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-tax-form-2020-300x113.jpg 300w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-tax-form-2020-768x288.jpg 768w, https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-tax-form-2020.jpg 940w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 940px) 100vw, 940px" loading="lazy" class="wp-image-476879 size-full" src="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/irs-tax-form-2020.jpg" alt="" width="940" height="353" /><figcaption id="caption-attachment-476879" class="wp-caption-text">IRS Form 1040 for the year 2020.</figcaption></figure> 
  
  <p>
    <!-- growjs zone placement 31 -->
    
    <ins> <ins class="growjs-placement"></ins> </ins> <!-- end of growjs zone placement -->
  </p>
  
  <p>
    Shehan Chandrasekera, Head of Tax Strategy at tax software company Cointracker, explained that “The revised question only inquires about your taxable transactions compared to the much broader scope of the 2020 version.”
  </p>
  
  <p>
    He opined, “Although these changes have no big impact on your taxes, it hints at what the IRS has learned from the 2020 version and the direction it’s heading,” elaborating:
  </p>
  
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Under the revised question, you don’t have to check ‘Yes’ if you send cryptocurrency in between wallets/exchanges or acquire them, which are both non-taxable transactions.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  
  <p>
    <em><strong>What do you think about the new crypto question on the tax form? Let us know in the comments section below.</strong></em>
  </p>
</div>
